// `src/unicode/class_range.npk` — ONE RANGE OF CODEPOINTS, OR OF BYTES. Cycle 0.2.0, RX-222;
// `meta/specs/UNICODE.md` U-4.
//
// U-4'S TYPE, DECLARED AT ITS LAYER BEFORE ITS TABLES. A class is a list of these
// (`HIR.md` H-2's `Hir.ranges`), and so is every table cycle 0.3 generates here --
// "the same shape a class uses, so a property lookup and a class test are the same
// code" (U-4). `hir` sits to the left of `unicode` in `BUILD.md` B-16's diagram, so a
// type both use is declared HERE: a `ClassRange` declared in `src/hir/` could not be
// named by the tables, which may not import `hir`. Like the layer's entry, `unicode.npk`,
// it is written by hand; the tables beside it from cycle 0.3.0 are generated.
//
// INCLUSIVE AT BOTH ENDS: `lo` and `hi` are the first and the last member. A class's
// list is sorted and disjoint once cycle 0.2.3 normalises it (H-13); a range read from
// a HIR dump only has `lo <= hi`. Codepoints are `0 … 0x10FFFF`, surrogates excluded;
// under `(?-u)` a class is over bytes, `0 … 255`, and its node says so (H-4a's
// `HIR_FLAG_BYTE`), not the range.
//
// It owns nothing, so a `Vec` holds it, and it derives `Copy`, which every `Vec`
// element must (`Vec<T: Copy>`, RX-188).
//
// LAYERING (`BUILD.md` §6, B-16). Imports nothing.
mod:class_range;

#[derive(Copy)]
pub struct:ClassRange = {
    uint32:lo;      // the first member
    uint32:hi;      // the last member, never below `lo`
};
